--TEST--
An `array<string>` reaches an `array<int|string>` parameter, and the callee's write through that widened view lands in its own copy — `rule:types/arrays`
--FILE--
<?nvs
class Widening {
    // The parameter declares the union and the caller below holds one member
    // of it, which is `rule:types/arrays`' covariant read. It is sound here
    // for the reason the rule gives: an array is a copy-on-write **value**,
    // so the append on the next line is a write to this function's own array
    // and there is no aliased storage for the caller to read it back out of.
    public static function joined(array<int|string> $items): string {
        $items[] = "added";
        string $out = "";
        foreach ($items as int|string $value) {
            $out = $out . ($value as string) . ";";
        }
        return $out;
    }
}

array<string> $names = ["ada", "grace"];
echo Widening::joined($names), "\n";

// The caller's array kept both its length and its element type: being read
// through a wider view is not a restamp of the array that was read.
echo Core\Arr::count($names), "\n";
string $first = $names[0];
echo $first, "\n";
--EXPECT--
ada;grace;added;
2
ada
